diagnosis_text:
A full end-to-end inspection of the simple_bgp topology finds no fault at any
layer. The eBGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is in
Established state, has been stable since lab start, and has exchanged exactly
one prefix in each direction with zero notifications or resets. Router1
correctly installs 200.1.1.0/24 via router2 (193.10.11.2), and router2
correctly installs 195.11.14.0/24 via router1 (193.10.11.1). Both host
interfaces are up and correctly addressed: pc1 holds 195.11.14.2/24 and pc2
holds 200.1.1.2/24. A live ping from pc1 to pc2 at 200.1.1.2 returns 4/4
packets with 0% loss and ~1.3 ms round-trip time, confirming full data-plane
connectivity in both directions. BGP configurations on both routers are
consistent with the intended design — correct neighbor addresses, correct
remote-AS values, correct `network` advertisement statements, and no
route-maps or filters that could silently drop or suppress prefixes. There is
no evidence of a transient flap, misconfiguration, or interface error. The
symptom reported in ticket #9001 (pc1 unable to reach pc2) is not reproducible
from the live lab; the network is operating correctly. No corrective action is
required. The ticket should be closed as "no fault found" and the end user
asked to retest from their machine, as the issue may have been transient or
client-side.
=== END ===
